/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/elyncia/Elyncia_02_The_Lares_DreamNet.md:39:The result became Web 3: the Lares DreamNet—a semi-sapient, hypercontextual infosphere composed of self-correcting astral engines and distributed personality spirits known as Lares. Each Lares governs a node—local or planetary—and serves both as a guide and gatekeeper. Some Lares shrines house benevolent muses. Others, inscrutable archivists. A few turn outright hostile. But the Hermetic Protocols bind them all, and Hephestus’s divine hammers code-etched every one.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/tools/empty-room.mjs:5:// registered uri-path — so doctype, frame-shape, bcc and the address gates all read it green, and the
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember.mem:59:The thief holds the floor key, every bare row, both slot trees and every sealed row, and no route. The thief stands the floor gates and speaks as the place where the place's own key speaks (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>). To lift a copy to SERVING the thief needs embers to answer an ask from that copy, and no byte at rest tells an ember the copy from the hearth: the copy holds the same floor key, the same pins and the same serving tree. So no ember answers the copy unattended. A hearth with no freshness anchor draws no unattended answer at all; a hearth with one keeps its hardware-sealed key in its own hardware, and the copy cannot present the attestation each ember checks. The copy's ask therefore waits for Mele's confirm on every ember the slot needs, and the prompt shows the hearth's own pet-name, as it would for the hearth: a person who knows the hearth stands where it stood, or who expected no raise, refuses. A copy raised by a person's confirm opens the serving tier: the gate's memory of withdrawals, the public halves that check walkers' grants, the walkers' carriage and the raise ledger, all ciphertext, public halves or signed events, and no root, seat key, face key, content key, minting secret or plaintext.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember.mem:84:The serving tier holds exactly the rows the serving gate and blind carriage read and write for parties already standing, and nothing beside them, and it holds no minting secret. The serving gate stands as the third gate tier: its bundle derives from SK under its own registered domain, and the floor and sovereign tiers stand untouched (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>). A pin of the serving route moves only by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step; no quiet period ages it out.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember.mem:115:* ''No act's signature.'' The custody root rests shut, so no veil, no Keyhive signer, no hosting key and no seat key stands. No roll, no hosting act, no invite mint, no grant, no dyad act, no quorum signature, no seal act. The serving gate's own bundle holds a handshake signing key, as every gate bundle does: it signs that gate's handshake and no act.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember.mem:165:# ''The ember dials the hearth's ember route.'' The route stands at the floor tier: its bundle derives from the floor key, so a locked hearth answers it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>). The route seats only a dialer whose handshake proves the transport key a standing ember pin names; every other dialer, a device of the person's own fleet included, draws the silence every floor gate gives. A floor gate seats no fleet lane, and this route seats none: it seats an ember pin.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember.mem:220:* ''The per-Nexus keys'': a face's proof key rests cold on the root holder, and a vessel's face-scoped key rests hot under the VK, so a serving hearth presents as no face at any Nexus and signs no hand-over. Its gate verifies a hand-over a presenter carries, since that act stands public and signed.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember.mem:222:* ''The sealed store's content keys'' stay in the daemon island's holder, which stands only on an unlocked vessel. SERVING mints, rolls and opens none. A roll a counted raise owes waits for the next human unlock, and the gates close on the shadow at once.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember.mem:251:* ''The ember route and its exchange frame'' (train E): a floor route name, its pin, the dialer's fleet frontier with the edge acts it carries, the ask (slot, pin, blinded point, raise nonce, standing, ordinal) and the answer, inside the floor gate's encrypted channel. Live: a wire frame.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember.mem:270:# ''The serving gate.'' A third gate tier, the serving route, its bundle derived from SK; the floor and sovereign tiers stand untouched. A pin moves only by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step. Reopens on proof that a gate pin decides no authority anywhere.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/sdm/ha.ka.ba/sdm/mount-points/structure.mem:42:A site-seated Power activates from the place. Reach, standing, or a maintained relationship may gate it — the shrine checks standing, not inventory (open pressure from mana-kapu). A mobile Site carries its seated Powers as it moves.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/grammar-invariants.mem:35:''EXCLUSION POWER IS BOUNDED BY THE EXCLUDER'S MARKET SHARE.'' Three fields built this gate correctly and three retreated under deployment pressure — the X.509 criticality MUST suspended by ballot; SOAP's `mustUnderstand` answered by //just ignore those//; BGP replacing session-reset with ignore (RFC 5492). ''MLS held because it gates at the GROUP boundary'', where the members are the whole relevant population and the roster stays local. So the gate reads deployable at ''Lararium and Nexus'' scale and predicts the same retreat at ''DreamNet'' scale — which lands exactly on the standing no-roster ruling, since a bilateral contract per operator keeps the check bilateral and peer-local.
