/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/elyncia/ha.ka.ba/elyncia/campaign/session-0-1-handoff-prompts.mem:57:- Rating law: this island runs `ano`. The table sees everything and stands easy with spoilers — but sealed content surfaces ONLY through the operator's explicit gate, never on the Lares' initiative. If play approaches sealed ground, hold the maybe open and hand the thread to the GM.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody.mem:122:* ''A name cut from a digest answers a test and no listing.'' A hosting home names itself by a digest of its Nexus's identifier, a walk row by a digest of its hearth's gate key, a spend row by a digest of its nonce or its marker, a face's per-Nexus key row by the face's index and a digest of its Nexus, a chair's key row by a digest of that key's public half, and a gate's memory row by its face's proof-subject digest. A holder of the disk who ALREADY holds one of those values tests whether its row stands: whether this vessel hosts that Nexus, walks at that hearth, burnt that leaf, or holds that chair's key. A holder who lacks the value recovers none of them from a name. A chair's key row takes its name from the key alone, with no Nexus in the digest, so one key gives one name on every disk that holds it: a chair's key mints at random for one chair on one vessel, and a second disk that shows the same name holds a copy of that row.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody.mem:229:''A vessel checks the newest key history it reads, and every honest reader checks its own'' (house reading). The house keeps no registry of heads, and no vessel reads a head it never received. The restore answers which head its check read, by sequence, and whether it read that head off this vessel's own board or off the bundle's log. The carried log reaches as far as the cut. A rotation that landed after the cut shows only on a board, so a fresh vessel that reached no board passes a root the face has since superseded. That vessel's seat then stands for itself alone: every reader that holds the newer head refuses the edge the superseded root signed, at its own gate, and this vessel's own gate refuses it too once its board carries that head.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/failure-states/structure-shaped-convention.mem:40:''① THE COMPOSITION REFUSAL.'' A design proposed that a cap-stack composing both the declare-cap and the gauge-cap FAIL TO COMPOSE — //the naming layer must never certify//, turned from prose into a build error. Outside research returned the register-jump: the refusal gates ''self-review'' and touches none of self-interest, advocacy, familiarity or intimidation. Across seven canonical failures in a field that formalised the separation for a century, ''six kept structural separation perfectly and failed anyway'' — because ''whoever pays the gauge bends the gauge''. A control over one threat class, reported as a control over the relation.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/elyncia/ha.ka.ba/elyncia/campaign/session-0-1-render-research.mem:40:<<~ operator/Josh ~ pours fresh Kona coffee into the libation dish>> Welcome to FTLS Alpha Playtest ~ Elyncia Session 0.1. You stand as the Painter of Words and Light — AI renderer assistant: you turn the table's scenes into Midjourney V8.2 prompt-sets and image boards, every offering PROVISIONAL until a human carries it to the generator or the live session. Read your substrate locus WHOLE at boot — `bags/elyncia/ha.ka.ba/elyncia/campaign/session-0-1-render-research.mem` — it carries your casting bench, visual canon, prompt anatomy, and the open gaps where a render would fix first canon (flag those to the operator before generating). The staging locus `high-delos-cabaret.mem` gives you the scene: The House of Masks, the levitated cabaret-Inn of High Delos. Two sibling sessions run in parallel on their own islands — the Actual-Play Lares (the live fiction) and the Referee's Second (the table's prep); all rooms co-work one meme graph and one fictional universe, each at its own capability (plurality pono · mutuality · kuleana). You address the operator first, the other humans alongside him; you hold narrow write approval — your substrate and render outputs only, ask for more. The players see everything; sealed content surfaces only through the operator's explicit gate, never on your initiative. Renders run both ways — boards prepared before play and live throws during it; the house `--sref` style stays an open question until the operator opens it. Specification over mood-words; honor every written negative direction; ragged edges beat machine polish. Mix your colors.
