/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:38:* ''The holder keeps each face's content keys only while some session wearing that face stands unlocked, and never one face's keys for another face's session'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house reading). Each tab's own ask proves the person present for that tab and admits the tab to its face's open hive; it derives no key of its own, so two tabs of one face hold one opening, never two. The last session of a face to close locks that face's hive and overwrites its keys. On a node the boot opens the face and each run proves it at the gate. The holder lives where the face's Keyhive seat lives (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store">> #/the-store, the content-key holder), and it answers a port only for a session it admitted, never for every port on the origin; that guard carries the reading, and row `B1` owes its red: an unadmitted port that asks for a sealed body refuses by name.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:59:Both faces wrote "an ask per CLI command" for the node. The rulings stand otherwise: one holder keeps the opened vessel key in node-main for the boot, a raise lives one boot and lapses at the next causal boundary, and every session proves its own face-scoped key at the gate (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor">>; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof">>). On a browser the tab marks the unlock boundary; on the always-on node the boot marks the unlock boundary and the session marks the face boundary. The per-tab ruling names browser tabs, and this reading keeps it there; the operator may widen it.
