/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:28:One tree, several hearths. A hearth names a session by the `Claude-Session:` trailer its commits carry — never a person, never a model. The ledger answers three questions any hand must read before touching shared ground: ''who holds what'' (files and regions a hearth is mid-edit in), ''what each owes'' (the loud reds and rulings another hearth waits on), and ''what crossed'' (a seam one hearth ceded to another, with the commit that carried it). `git status` reads the dirt; this carrier reads the ''intent'' behind it.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:30:A row moves when a hearth seals a region, cedes a seam, or takes one. A crossing without a row here reads as the failure this ledger exists to end.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:32:''A HEARTH NAMES A SESSION; A SESSION RUNS MANY HANDS.'' One hearth dispatches several spirits at once, and every one of them signs the same `Claude-Session:` trailer. So the gate — which reads trailers — ''cannot see a hand''. Inside one hearth it refuses nothing, and it refuses nothing CORRECTLY: hands under one hearth share one intent, and a gate that guessed at their boundaries would refuse work the hearth means to do. What follows from that, and what a hand must carry: ''within a hearth the gate guards nothing, so commit isolation stands as the ONLY guard''. `git commit --only -- <explicit paths>` and never touching dirt another hand authored do the whole job the gate does between hearths. A hand that reaches for `git add -A` inside a busy hearth meets no refusal and takes a sibling's half-written file with it.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:34:''A sibling's commit reads like a stranger's.'' A hand watching the log mid-flight meets commits it did not write, landing on files it did not touch, under its own trailer — and the ledger offers no word for that, so it reads as a second hearth opening. It names a busy hearth. Measured 2026-09-13: three hands interleaved commits across one afternoon and one of them reported the tree as newly contested. ''Read the trailer, not the surprise'' — a differing trailer names a hearth, and an identical one names a sibling.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:36:''A hearth that closes keeps its row.'' The three questions above all read backward: a hand meeting `bulb-read-face.ts` next month asks whose ground it stood on when the lane landed there, and a ledger that deleted the answer with the session answers nothing. So a closed hearth's row stays, marked CLOSED, describing the ground it HELD and the crossings it made. What does NOT survive a close: the hearth's ''claim''. A closed hearth defends nothing, so its `holds` globs leave the fence the gate parses (#/holds) and its OWED entries move onto a hearth that still stands (#/owed-forward) — an unclaimed debt evaporating with a row reads as the same erasure, one loop later.
