/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:82:The table above reads the ground in prose, which a hand reads and a gate cannot. This block names the same ground in ''repo-relative globs'', keyed by the trailer a hearth's commits carry, so `tools/hearths-gate.mjs` can refuse a commit that reaches into another hearth's ground before the ledger carries a crossing row for it. The prose stands as the meaning; the globs stand as its edge.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:84:A glob that matches nothing belongs to no hearth — the gate names only what a commit actually touches. A path claimed by two hearths reads as a crossing in flight, and the gate says so rather than picking a winner.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:86:''A closed hearth defends nothing, so its globs leave this fence.'' The fence states a ''claim against other hands'', and a session that has ended presses none. Leaving a closed hearth's globs here would refuse the only hearth left standing from ground nobody contests — an outage wearing a safeguard's clothes, which the gate's own header names as the shape it must not take. The ground the closed hearths held stands recorded twice over: in prose in the table above, and verbatim in the `holds-closed` fence below, which the gate does not parse.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:88:!! What one hearth does to the gate
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:90:''Under a single hearth `tools/hearths-gate.mjs` refuses nothing, and it does so honestly rather than switched off.'' Its `verdict` refuses only a path that lands in a hold whose `trailer` differs from the committing hearth's; with one row, no path can. The pass note counts paths on the committing hearth's own ground separately from unheld paths; it does not attribute every file to a hearth. A commit carrying no trailer passes with the operator-seat note. The live fence above carries no hold at all, so every commit passes in silence until a hearth seats one. A `[[hold]]` row that parses without a quoted trailer or without a glob defends nothing, and the gate names each such row on every commit until the ledger mends it: a fence that tore whole never reads as a fence that holds nothing.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:92:''Leave the gate installed.'' It arms again the moment a second `[[hold]]` row appears — no code moves, no hook re-installs; the ledger serves as the configuration, and the gate reads it fresh at every commit. Deleting the tool would make the next hearth's opening a build task instead of a table edit.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:94:''The witness reads two ledgers, and the ledger says why.'' `tools/hearths-gate-witness.sh` drives the gate against ''the real ledger'' by design — a witness reading a fixture proves the parser rather than the rows the tree stands on. A one-hold ledger can produce no crossing, so its ★ RED vector would have passed by having nothing to refuse: a gate wearing a check it never earned. The witness reads the live fence's count and, at one hold, runs its ARMING vectors against a fixture gathered from ''this ledger's own `[[hold]]` blocks, live and closed, in one live fence'' — the exact shape the tree takes when a second hearth opens — while every pass-through CONTROL keeps reading the real ledger. It PRINTS which ledger each half read. Nothing in `hearths-gate.mjs` moved.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:96:''The pass note speaks only when something stands held'' (`hearths-gate.mjs`'s header): a pass over ground no hold names prints nothing, so a tree with no live hold commits in silence and the one line that does print stays worth reading. A gate that says less only by that law keeps the reader it needs.
