/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:208:# ''The torn charter'' — the founding hearth measured a live re-seat bypass in the MEME hearth's `nexus-seal.ts` and cured it in place rather than handing back a bug report over a one-line guard. `readNexusDoc` answers null for BOTH "no charter stands" and "a charter stands and reads torn"; `sealSeat` folds that null into a founding scaffold carrying no `sealLineage`, so the re-seat guard (`doc.sealLineage && …`) short-circuits on undefined and a rotated-but-unreadable chain re-genesises itself, stranding every antigen entry rooted on the head it replaces. Measured: rotate to epoch1, tear the fences, re-seat returned 0. Files crossed: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/nexus-seal.ts` (five added lines ahead of the existing guard; no line moved) — beside `packages/lararium-node/src/nexus-doc.ts` + its barrel (`nexusCharterStands`, the founding hearth's own ground) and the CLI's lineage test. The ABSENT case still seats, pinned by a CONTROL. Law: a SAFETY guard that another hearth's file defeats gets cured with a row, never left standing while the row is negotiated — and the row names the shape so the holding hearth can re-cut it if the seam belongs elsewhere.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:209:# ''The last split home'' — a founding-hearth spirit migrated nine CLI doors' help text into the one registry and hit the gate on the tenth: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/meme.ts`, the meme hearth's ground. It STOPPED, reverted its edit cleanly, and left the prepared patch beside a SELF-CLEARING exception in the weld (`HANDED_BACK = ["meme put"]`, with a second assertion that goes red the moment the split closes, so the hole retires itself rather than settling in). The coordinator later applied that patch under the operator's explicit approval; the exception fired exactly as designed and both it and its guard came out in the same commit. Files crossed: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/meme.ts` (a 34-line `const USAGE` deleted, `usage()` now reading `helpLines("meme")` — the registry already carried the door's words, only the module's copy remained). No behaviour moved; `lares meme` prints the same menu. Law: a spirit that meets the gate hands back a PATCH and a self-clearing weld, never a bug report — the exception then names its own retirement date instead of waiting on someone to remember it.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:210:# ''The offering door'' — the operator ruled `pluginsCid` and its caps OUT of the regenesis/kāhuli verbs and named the door: `lares nexus publish`. The whole `nexus` family stands on the meme hearth's ground, so building a door the operator named there crosses by construction rather than by reach. Files crossed: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/nexus-publish.ts` (NEW), `nexus.ts` (one dispatch case + two usage lines), `nexus-kahuli.ts` (the plugins REPORT removed, a POINTER left in its place), and the verb-reach snapshot that pins the three usage surfaces byte-for-byte. No kāhuli behaviour moved — the ratchet reads its own two regions exactly as before, and a CONTROL pins that the cut removed nothing else. STAGE 1 of three: the door READS; the signed offering + crossroads announce (stage 2) and the Lamplighters capability with its present-never-condemn check (stage 3) stand named in the door's own `--apply` hold. Law: a door the operator NAMES on another hearth's ground still gets a row — the row is what lets that hearth re-cut the seam later without re-deriving why the door exists.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:211:# ''The twice-spelled route'' — a founding-hearth scout measured two read-faces whose paths stood spelled in the server and again in the client with no shared constant, the regex hand-written on one side and the template literal on the other. The pair never runs in one process, so nothing forces the two to move together: two vessels on skewed builds 404 at each other, and a 404 reads as a peer being DOWN rather than as a rename that half-landed. The oracle pair sits wholly on founding ground (constants landed in MESH — node imports mesh, never the reverse). The BULB pair crosses: `packages/lararium-node/src/bulb-read-face.ts` stands on the meme hearth's ground while `kindle.ts` and the new `bulb-routes.ts` sit on the founding hearth's, and splitting the edit mid-law would have left that face holding an import for constants its own literals no longer matched. Files crossed: `bulb-read-face.ts` (six literals replaced by the shared spellings; no route moved, both integration welds green). Law: a tidy-up that crosses gets the SAME row a cure does — the row records that nothing moved, which is exactly what the holding hearth needs to know before re-reading its own face.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:212:# ''The narrowed claim'' — stage 3 of the offering landed wholly on founding ground (`@lararium/mesh` · plugin-offering · offering-antigen), and one SENTENCE in the meme hearth's `nexus-kahuli.ts` had to move with it. That header read that an operator's own collection "moves with no ratchet act at all" — true about RATCHETS, and readable as "ungoverned by design", which a new opt-in offering-antigen would have silently contradicted. The comment now narrows itself: the claim holds about ratchets and never held about consent, the governance sits OPT-IN per Nexus, and a condemned offering still overturns nobody's grammar. Files crossed: `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/nexus-kahuli.ts` (a comment alone; no behaviour, and its own tests plus the publish weld stay green). Law: when new law narrows an old claim, the claim gets narrowed IN THE SAME COMMIT — a reader who meets the old sentence after the new law lands has been told something false by omission, and "we added a gate elsewhere" never reaches them.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:213:# ''The stash'' — a meme-hearth spirit (Heraldry-Hand) ran `git stash` on the shared tree mid-flight, swallowing 61 files of two other hands' work; recovered by `stash show -p` + blob restore, `stash@{0}` left standing. Law: a brief names the ROAD (a throwaway worktree · `git show HEAD:<path>`), never only the wall.
