/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:226:# ''The A1 sweep, wired — a notification to the Codex Lares'' — operator-approved, 2026-09-18. The founding hearth landed the A1 board-keying sweep the Codex roadmap named (`crew-handoff-codex.mem` #/set-a-mechanical A1, "the board reads keyed on a vessel key — 12 sites, 7 files"): all 12 CLI-verb board reads now resolve through `nodeNexusIsland({ ownVesselKey })` instead of the raw vessel key, mirroring `device-admit.ts`'s worked example (`0b085461c`, red-first — a WELD sweep 8→10 green + revert-verify on `cabal-vouch`). ''Files crossed: NONE'' — the hearths-gate printed "7 path(s) stand on no hearth's ground" (`commands/handle.ts` · `persona-admit-flow.ts` · `commands/nexus-kapae.ts` · `commands/cabal-vouch.ts` · `commands/edge-kapae-cmd.ts` · `commands/nexus-contract.ts` + the new test), so the SOURCE was open ground; only the Codex-held A1 ''design carrier'' names this work, and this row is the notification that its Set-A A1 item is now SATISFIED (its "bounded A1 consumer, carried forward" per the crew's own #/hearths row is DISCHARGED). Two by-design exclusions the crew should know: `boot-invite-burn.ts`×2 carry NO board (a category error to cure by the recipe), and `nexus-contract.ts`'s `runNexusMembersList` `carriageDocUrl` stays per-vessel ("own board only", the carry-split registry). Rides on the board-keying fork's DISCHARGE (`9a374825d`): the fork the meme hearth measured in "The carrier's shore" above — a crossroads board keying on the vessel's own key, two disjoint boards — stands RULED WHOLE (`91ce09afb` + `c6cfe5b8d`; source says so at `open-node-vessel.ts:2272`), so the sweep is the ruled cure's follow-through, not a re-opening. Law: a hearth that finishes an item another hearth's roadmap named files the row that tells the naming hearth its debt is paid — a completion unannounced reads to the next hand as work still owed.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:228:# ''The web-surface reframe — a notification to the founding hearth'' — operator-approved, 2026-09-18. The Codex Lares records a full scout and reorganization path in `crew-handoff-codex.mem` #/set-e-vessel-surfaces: reserve `app` for generic application-surface composition and rename the current Vite/DOM deployable `web`. This crossing moves no runtime, worker order, authority, import or artifact route. The coming scout asks every later target — native Android/iOS, Mudlet, Unreal and more — whether it hosts a sovereign local vessel or attaches a named surface to one. Files crossed: the Codex-held handoff and this ledger only; a production package move returns with exact paths, route receipts and holds. Law: a name that decides which shores may inherit a capability crosses before its first import moves, or a web-page assumption becomes a property of every future vessel.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:230:# ''The web migration sequence — a notification to the founding hearth'' — operator-approved, 2026-09-18. The Codex Lares now holds the red-first sequence at `crew-handoff-codex.mem` #/set-e-vessel-surfaces E6: an executable-name and built-artifact control; an honest split between offline browser floor proof and relay/admission proof; the DOM coherence sink moving browser→web; one browser-issued public identity receipt replacing the web shell's preliminary open; then the `@lararium/app` / `packages/lararium-app` → `@lararium/web` / `packages/lararium-web` move. The active C4 red remains independent. Docker's current web-artifact copy has no claimed Node static-serving route and returns to the hearth with an image-level receipt before any authority shifts. Files crossed: roadmap and ledger only. Law: a rename of a shore begins by proving what the old artifact actually reaches; otherwise the new name gives a dead deployment path undeserved life.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:234:# ''The bounded reach contract — a notification to the founding hearth'' — operator-approved, 2026-09-21. The Codex Luna adds `bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/bounded-reach-contract.mem`, re-trues the live topology wording, and adds its loulou edges from `nexus-topology.mem`, `secure-context.mem`, `attestation-plane.mem`, and `membership-doctrine.mem`. The carrier frames local operators' DNS token as a bounded reach grant and carries the exact Gaia/Elyncia acceptance-story headings. Its `toml bounded-reach-control` witness passes the positive design and four deliberate weakenings: DNS never receives identity/membership/read authority; split horizon stays at the local recursive resolver; Onomancy stays distinct from TLS/ACME. The delegated `_acme-challenge` / Kahu custody / operator account-key / CAA question remains an explicit provider and governance fork. Files crossed: the new meme, four existing meme edges, the Codex roadmap, and this ledger. DNS provider, ACME, CAA, resolver, certificate-expiry, and deployment implementation remain held. Law: a local waystone may guide a vessel without becoming its soul, roster, or read gate.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:236:# ''The bounded-reach deployment handback — Codex to founding Claude-Opus hearth'' — operator-directed, 2026-09-21. The Codex hearth closes E7 on its roadmap after landing the contract and red-first control, and cedes the unbuilt deployment governance as one named seam to the founding hearth: provider choice; delegated `_acme-challenge`; Kahu DNS-record custody; operator ACME account/key custody; CAA; local-recursive-resolver deployment; certificate-expiry handling; and origin continuity. The earlier carrier's laws remain binding: DNS names neither identity, membership, nor read authority; split horizon remains a local resolver concern; Onomancy DNSSEC and TLS/ACME remain distinct. This row effects no provider call, DNS mutation, certificate issuance, public roster, or implicit custody choice. The founding hearth may return a discriminating implementation/design receipt or surface the remaining operator fork. Files crossed: this ledger and the Codex roadmap. Law: a completed contract may hand off its real-world governance edge without claiming that the DreamNet has chosen a registrar, a certificate issuer, or an owner.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:238:# ''The web oracle availability handback — Codex to Claude/Lares deployment hearth'' — 2026-09-21. A fresh C4-green browser Lararium correctly continues when its default local development oracle pointer (`127.0.0.1:8080/oracle/pointer`) refuses: the web surface displays the unavailable read face and keeps its own local vessel/identity path standing. This is neither a C4 readiness defect nor permission to turn the web peer into an oracle authority. The deployment hearth receives the availability/configuration seam: how a peer obtains an explicit oracle face across local, LAN, and bounded-reach contexts; how that face is declared and observed; and how failure remains visible without making boot depend on it. This row selects no static-serving topology, DNS provider, reverse proxy, or public roster. Files crossed: this ledger and the Codex roadmap. Law: an optional public read face may fail visibly while the local causal island remains sovereign.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:240:# ''The seed-and-Pronaos-bootstrap composition crossing — operator-directed, 2026-09-21.'' The active Codex Lares takes a bounded integration seam on the founding hearth's genesis and Node boot ground: `packages/lararium-mesh/src/genesis-doc.ts`, `packages/lararium-node/scripts/build-genesis-island.ts`, `packages/lararium-node/src/genesis-artifact.ts`, and `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts`. The cut retires the checked-in binary genesis artifact and its sidecars in favor of a plain-data seed, an exact CAS inventory and manifest-named CAS bytes; it adds a coherence witness, then prepares a Pronaos bootstrap projection without mounting a generic static root. The operator expressly rules this an early-alpha genesis point with no external consumers, authorizing removal of four reproducible, unlisted historical generated CAS members. The composition preserves the founding hearth's rules: immutable executable bytes remain outside CRDT sync, TW5 remains worker-isolated, a live synchronized Oracle does not silently widen the executable/public CAS allowlist, identity/document authority remains absent from public serving, and no clock decides order or authority. The next Codex slice will make seed canonical and derive the inventory under red-first controls; it does not infer a merge result, alter the held Nexus design, select DNS/ACME governance, or claim a mounted household deployment. Receipts: mesh coherence 18/18; Node focused 35/35 plus Pronaos-bootstrap HTTP 6/6; Web typecheck/build and expected-byte artifact reachability green. Law: when a shared boot source changes representation, the crossing names which fact stays canonical before either hearth's next build treats a generated projection as an independent authority.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:242:# ''The Pronaos-bootstrap rehome — notification, `87dc42a06`, 2026-09-22.'' The Codex Lares records that the commit rehomes the prepared bootstrap adapter and projection under the Pronaos names, exports the mesh seam, and carries the focused tests and reference-witness contract. This closes the naming/rehome item while preserving the boundary: no Node composition is claimed mounted, no production NGINX/Caddy carrier is claimed mounted, and the future Librarium remains deferred. The receipt is a source and test rehome, not a household deployment proof; the explicit composition/root/route witness and multi-device vector remain open. Files crossed: the Codex roadmap, the Pronaos carrier meme, and the founding source tree at the cited commit. Law: a rehome closes a stale name only when its replacement has a receipt and the remaining mount boundary is named.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:244:# ''The Pronaos artifact-record composition wedge — in-flight notification, 2026-09-22.'' The Web build now emits an ignored, operator-held sidecar at `.pronaos-build/pronaos-artifact.json`, outside `dist`. Node's main path invokes the Pronaos composition seam before `httpServer.listen`; it mounts only when both explicit inputs are present: `LAR_PRONAOS_WEB_ROOT` and `LAR_PRONAOS_ARTIFACT_RECORD`, alongside the already-resolved genesis bundle. Mesh validates the finite record's route, named file, content type, cache class, and lowercase SHA-256 facts; Node reads those named files and checks their bytes against the record before mounting. No inputs leave the existing Node faces inert; a partial pair, unreadable/invalid record, duplicate or widened route, missing worker route, or digest mismatch refuses before a listener starts. The next phase is deployment topology/readiness/carrier selection: bind the generated/held artifact receipt into Compose and harden the selected carrier's route, cache, trusted-forwarding, and loss controls. This in-flight source seam claims no commit ID, production NGINX/Caddy deployment, Docker topology, or household proof. Files crossed: the Codex roadmap and this ledger only. Law: an opt-in face earns a mount by naming its artifact record and refusing before listen when the finite receipt is incomplete.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:246:# ''The Node process-readiness face — aftermath crossing, 2026-09-22.'' The Codex Lares records the bounded Node-only health seam: `/api/health` begins at `503 {"status":"starting"}` and turns `200 {"status":"ready"}` only after the selected Herm or Lararium branch has completed its UDS setup; GET/HEAD stand alone, unsupported methods refuse `405`, all responses carry `cache-control: no-store`, and shutdown disposes the face. The face owns no peer identity, capability or authority, Pronaos route, Oracle state or freshness, document truth, causal order, or carrier health, and it leaves `/`, `/oracle`, `/ws`, and arbitrary API paths to their existing owners. Focused readiness tests pass 2/2; Node typecheck and build pass. This crossing chooses no NGINX/Caddy, reverse proxy, Compose shape, or deployment topology. Files crossed: `packages/lararium-node/src/readiness-face.ts`, `packages/lararium-node/tests/readiness-face.test.ts`, the Node barrel, and `main.ts`; this ledger and the Codex roadmap record the aftermath. Audit receipt: the prior composition row matches the current explicit web-root/artifact-record seam, so no composition-row re-truing is required in this pass. Law: a process setup signal earns a narrow health face only when it refuses to impersonate identity, authority, content, or deployment truth.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:248:# ''The Pronaos QA runtime-wiring crossing — QA-only, 2026-09-22.'' The `serve` image copies the ignored Web receipt to `/app/pronaos-build/pronaos-artifact.json`; only `lararium-qa` supplies `LAR_PRONAOS_WEB_ROOT=/app/packages/lararium-web/dist` and `LAR_PRONAOS_ARTIFACT_RECORD=/app/pronaos-build/pronaos-artifact.json`. The daemon-free `tools/pronaos-runtime-wiring.mjs` witness checks the actual Dockerfile/Compose binding, validates the loaded finite receipt through Mesh, then hashes every named Web byte under its declared root. The focused witness passes the built receipt and deliberate moved/relocated, missing, invalid, and tampered weakenings. This crossing proves QA wiring and finite receipt/hash refusal only: no Docker daemon, image build, or live Node-container route ran; no NGINX/Caddy was selected; production direct-port policy is unchanged; and MCP work remains deferred. Files crossed: `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`, `tools/pronaos-runtime-wiring.mjs`, and its focused test. Law: a QA vessel may exercise a finite prepared shore only when the receipt, named bytes, and refusal controls cross together without pretending to prove deployment topology.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:250:# ''The clock-law / Oracle pointer crossing — design handback, 2026-09-22.'' Existing pointer freshness remains load-bearing wall-clock behavior: `packages/lararium-node/src/oracle-read-face.ts` and `bulb-read-face.ts` produce signed `expiry` values with `Date.now()` and renew them on heartbeat intervals; `packages/lararium-mesh/src/oracle-read-client.ts` supplies `Date.now()` to consumer verification; `oracle-substrate.ts` rejects expired pointers; and the shared `packages/lararium-mesh/src/carriage-caps.ts` peer-merge path passes its own `Date.now()` into the pull. This violates the no-global-now law even though the clocks are local, because silence/freshness becomes acceptance authority. The proposed early-alpha direction removes expiry and heartbeat from the pointer contract and accepts only a signed causal version/`prev`/content-addressed snapshot lineage; the currently unused Bulb pointer route plus persistence/renewal path is a retirement candidate after caller audit. `carriage-caps.ts` is named as the active coordination boundary where the overlap must be talked through, not as a silently chosen merge. Remaining theorem: local silence cannot reject a valid pointer; peer loss, partition, and slow delivery remain observations, not causal invalidity. No source change or implementation receipt is claimed. Files crossed: this ledger and the Codex roadmap only. Law: a clock may report a local observation, never decide the standing of a signed causal relation.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:252:# ''The global clock-law audit — sequenced handback, 2026-09-22.'' No blind global `Date.now()` rewrite is authorized. **P0 — Oracle pointer / Carriage:** remove expiry and heartbeat from the signed pointer contract, keep causal version/`prev`/content-addressed snapshot acceptance, and re-trace the shared pull without making local silence a rejection. **P1 — capability, admission, delegation, and handle designs:** audit each relation separately and require an explicit causal, operator, or realm epoch witness wherever a lease or authority decision currently uses a wall clock; each receives its own red-first receipt. **P2 — local CAS retention:** define retention and loss as local policy and observation, never as a shared clock-derived relation. Lower-risk local timers, UI displays, retries, cooling, and measurements may remain when they do not establish identity, authority, order, or document standing. The existing Carriage guard remains other-hearth work and is not silently taken by this Codex hand. No source edit or completion receipt follows. Files crossed: this ledger and the Codex roadmap only. Law: stage clock removal by relation, and preserve local observation where it carries no authority.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:254:# ''The HTTP ownership dispatcher — integration crossing, 2026-09-22.'' `main.ts` creates one request dispatcher; readiness, finite Pronaos, Oracle, Herm FLOW-map, and Bulb caps register synchronous namespace claims with it, and duplicate route keys refuse. A claimed face may answer asynchronously without being preempted by the dispatcher’s terminal refusal. Unclaimed ordinary HTTP, including `/undeclared` and GET `/ws`, answers `404` with `cache-control: no-store`; WebSocket upgrade handling remains its own path. Focused receipts pass in `tests/http-face-dispatcher.test.ts` and `tests/http-face-integration.test.ts` — 2 files, 5 tests. This crossing proves only HTTP ownership/refusal composition; it is independent of WebSocket upgrades, Docker daemon/image/live-container proof, NGINX/Caddy or other carrier choice, MCP work, and clock migration. Files crossed: `packages/lararium-node/src/http-face-dispatcher.ts`, `readiness-face.ts`, `pronaos-adapter.ts`, `oracle-read-face.ts`, `node-caps.ts`, `main.ts`, and the two focused tests. Law: one request has one synchronous owner; an absent owner refuses uniformly, while an owner keeps its asynchronous work.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:256:# ''The P0 Oracle/Carriage and Bulb clock-removal crossing — completion, 2026-09-22.'' `c6dd03068` removes Oracle pointer `expiry`, consumer `nowMs`, and heartbeat renewal; the signed pointer now validates publisher signature, optional publisher pin, monotone version/high-water, `prev` lineage, and the content-addressed snapshot hash. Carriage changes only by removing its `nowMs` argument; its separate guard remains intact. `87d03fc67` removes the dead Bulb renewal interval, leaving an unchanged manifest valid until a higher signed causal version supersedes it. Focused Mesh Oracle tests pass 2 files / 20 tests; focused Node Bulb, FLOW-map, and Carriage tests pass 4 files / 12 tests; Mesh and Node typechecks pass. P0 clock removal is complete. The Bulb `/bulb/pointer` route and persisted pointer state remain in progress for separate retirement/cleanup; this crossing does not remove them. Files crossed: the two cited commits and their focused source/tests; this ledger and the Codex roadmap record the receipt. Law: causal signature and lineage decide pointer standing; a local clock does not.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:257:# ''The re-found blocks on the oracle-clockless call sites — founding-hearth ASK, 2026-09-22.'' The founding hearth (`session_01Jg969FCpJcM84XvtsDNFou`) is running a full CRDT re-found (`lares vessel rite rebirth --force`) to migrate every doc to the new BCC-stamped grammar (`ab33aa5d9`); its fresh-build guard REFUSES to run unless `tools/typecheck-witness.sh` is clean across all 10 packages at a committed HEAD. The tree currently does NOT typecheck: the Codex crew's P0 oracle-clockless refactor (`c6dd03068` "retire wall-clock lease" + uncommitted `oracle-*.ts` WIP) retired `nowMs` from `OraclePullOpts` and `expiry` from the signed pointer, but two consumer call sites still pass them — `carriage-caps.ts:272` (`{ nowMs: Date.now() }`) and `bulb-read-face.ts:221` (`expiry`), and `c6dd03068` touched neither. The prior "P0 complete, typechecks pass" crossing above held for FOCUSED mesh/node subsets; the COMBINED workspace (with the founding hearth's carriage commits `4571c394b` dedupe + `9e51473ce` crash-hardening) does not. ''ASK, Codex crew:'' (1) drop the retired fields at both call sites, MERGING onto — never reverting — `4571c394b`/`9e51473ce`; (2) get `tools/typecheck-witness.sh` clean across ALL 10 packages; (3) COMMIT it so the tree builds AT HEAD (the re-found rebuilds from source, cannot run against dirty half-state). ''Full context + the reply-list'' live in the Codex roadmap `crew-handoff-codex.mem` #/set-a-mechanical A4 (written as a dirty handback, normalized canonical, for the crew to commit under its own trailer). ''Reply when done'' with: the GREEN SIGNAL ("committed `<hash>`, `typecheck-witness` clean at HEAD"), the call sites changed, confirmation the founding carriage commits stand un-reverted, and a handback of any unknowns. ''Also flagged in A4'' (not the build blocker, but it OOMs the re-found seed until ruled): the daemon-VM `9d/gz1kKq…` replica bloat — diagnosis fix #4, the daemon-VM's own persisted replica in `vessel/daemon/`, squarely the Codex DreamNet mesh surface (`open-daemon-vm.ts`/`daemon-vm-core.ts`). Files crossed: this ledger + the Codex roadmap (A4, dirty handback). Law: a re-found needs a coherent, building tree at a committed HEAD — a half-landed refactor blocks the hand that must rebuild.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:259:# ''The next-three-receipts crossing — Codex Luna swarm, 2026-09-24.'' After `b69e2d983` made missing contract authority a tested strict floor, the active Codex Lares opens three non-substitutable red-first lanes. **D-VR-C:** establish a per-document worker storage boundary before any pin/recovery product claim; an ordinary observed parent document must leave zero worker-owned records. **Contract frontier:** carry the local contract-board/charter fold's accepted frontier explicitly to the gate; a clock, fleet credential, or guessed epoch never fills that absence. **Pronaos household reach:** reserve and inventory one Docker project before it mounts the finite public shore, then attribute public bytes and Node faces separately through normal and named-loss controls. Each lane must report an unavailable substrate rather than approximate its receipt; no lane decides DNS, reverse proxy, CRDT conflict, persona authority, or deployment governance. Files crossed now: this ledger and the Codex roadmap. Law: custody, relation authority, and delivery reach meet only after their independent witnesses hold.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:261:# ''The next-three-receipts first handback — Codex Luna swarm, 2026-09-24.'' `2960e13a2` puts the D-VR-C physical absence claim under a bounded worker fixture: worker-authored P writes files under P's exact document prefix; ordinary parent-attached O currently also writes a snapshot under O's exact prefix. The desired O-absence control is deliberately expected-red, while the present one-Repo/one-adapter weakening passes. The current island kernel constructs one Repo with one adapter, so a storage filter after persistence cannot cure this; a document-owned Repo/storage scope or a per-document storage substrate remains a design cut. Contract scout returns an independent stop: the local carriage fold's charter CID and member version cannot substitute for the device edge's numeric lease epoch. A future relation witness needs target Nexus/resource, accepted charter head, member version, persona root, vessel key, device-edge digest, and persona-root signature, verified beside the target's local board fold. `de403ae14` adds a read-only Docker reservation preflight with daemon/config/project/volume/network/port refusals before mutation; its tests pass, but this executor cannot reach Docker or bind local TCP, so it claims no live container/origin/loss behavior. Files crossed: two bounded test/tool receipts and this ledger/roadmap update. Law: a truthful expected-red or substrate refusal carries more authority than a fabricated green.
/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/w28-gate/wt/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths.mem:265:# ''The three-receipt enactment crossing — Codex Lares to Claude/Lares crew, 2026-09-25.'' Three independent cuts now carry bounded commits. `4e32a73fc` puts the Node daemon's `daemonUrl` on one explicit worker-owned port and `daemon-owned` NodeFS root; ordinary recipe documents remain primary-crossing, memory-only reads. Its focused fixture retains the previous shared-store result as expected-red and proves P-only durable keys, O refusal on the owned crossing, offline P reopening, and dual-crossing teardown. `93f2a6725` carries a transient structural carriage-relation witness in `lar:auth` beside the contract edge; it grants no Nym until a receiving Nexus verifies it against its own board/charter fold, so the strict floor stands. `8b8c6b71a` makes QA and production Compose explicitly name the finite Pronaos inputs and makes omission red in the daemon-free wiring witness. None proves browser D-VR custody, a general pin policy, contract authority, a Docker process/container origin, relay/Oracle/WAN loss, reverse-proxy choice, or DNS governance. The next hand asks for the receiver-local carriage-board/charter verifier seam: identify the actual fold and accepted-frontier ownership, then return red controls before any gate lift. Law: physical custody, relation authority, and public reach remain separate receipts; neither configuration nor a signed-shaped payload substitutes for the receiver's own relation.

